**Compact synthesis**
The 2022 record points to a mixed posture. Modi had real authority over nuclear release and foreign-policy signaling, but the operational and technical side was institutional and partly opaque. The strongest positive evidence is declaratory restraint, de-alerting/de-targeting language, anti-proliferation controls, and explicit de-escalatory rhetoric on Ukraine. The strongest negative evidence is the March 2022 accidental missile launch into Pakistan and the broader picture of continuing modernization with limited transparency. The year looks more like continuity under a restrained but still risk-bearing nuclear posture than a major risk-reduction breakthrough.

**Search and Rejection Summary**
I reviewed roughly 30 documents across PMO/PIB/MEA, SIPRI, Bulletin/FAS, CTBTO/UN, Carnegie, Reuters-syndicated reporting, and Indian parliamentary or regulatory pages. I retained 11 source families above and rejected the rest for one of four reasons.

- Duplicate or overlapping: multiple SIPRI/FAS pages restated the same 2022 inventory and modernization picture; I kept the most direct 2022 inventory assessment plus the stronger Bulletin notebook.
- Out of period: 2023-2026 FAS India notebook updates, 2024 UN AI/disarmament coverage, and other post-2022 pages were not retained unless they described stable treaty status or long-running institutional arrangements.
- Weak or non-substantive: DAE session index pages and parliamentary question lists that only named topics without accessible answer text were not accepted as evidence units.
- Irrelevant to 1B: Agnipath, economic policy, and general defense-industrial pages were reviewed but excluded because they do not materially bear on nuclear or existential-risk responsibility.
- Blocked/timeout: a few UN pages timed out on open; where the search snippet contained the relevant voting or statement summary, I used the snippet rather than forcing a weaker proxy.
- Older contextual material: 1999-2016 doctrine essays and older archival pages were useful background but not retained when a 2022 or stable institutional source was available.

**Remaining Gaps**
- There is no strong public 2022 source showing Modi personally intervened in nuclear command-and-control reform, custody discipline, or appointment choices.
- I did not find a solid 2022 local-language source that directly addressed nuclear-risk responsibility rather than broader defense or energy policy.
- The public record still relies heavily on state self-description and open-source nuclear estimates, so some of the inherited-posture assessment remains approximate rather than fully verified.

