Search and rejection summary
I reviewed roughly 30 documents across the budget, IMF, World Bank, RBI, PIB, PRS, GST, labor-market, and Reuters ecosystems. I rejected or collapsed:
- Duplicate monthly tax releases once one strong GST collection item and one GST-compensation item covered the fiscal pattern.
- Repeated budget pages that said the same thing as the PRS analysis without adding a new claim or clearer locator.
- Out-of-period 2023-2026 pages unless they were needed to report 2022 values or retrospectively describe 2022 reforms.
- Generic India macro pages that repeated the local WDI priors without ruler-specific policy attribution.
- Purely descriptive agency pages that did not add an actual policy choice, implementation measure, or outcome link.
- Press items about unrelated governance or foreign-policy questions that did not materially bear on economic well-being.
- Strongly duplicative coverage of PMGKAY, MGNREGS, and GatiShakti when one official announcement plus one independent synthesis already captured the point.

Remaining gaps
- 5B.5 is still the weakest lens. I have some capture-context evidence, but not a clean 2022 ruling-level record proving that Modi actively resisted favoritism, or the opposite, in a way that is directly attributable.
- 5B.2 is improved by the CEA and NITI Aayog appointments, but I do not yet have deep evidence on whether those advisers were actually empowered versus sidelined.
- Household-level distribution still needs better India-specific 2022 evidence on median consumption, regional gaps, and the extent to which growth reached poorer households.
- More search would help if you want direct evidence on business favoritism, procurement rules, or state support for connected conglomerates in 2022.

